(x=0, y=2)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span></span><span><span> and </span></span><span><span><span><span><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\def\A{\red{A}}
\def\B{\blue{B}}
\def\C{\green{C}}
(x=2, y=-4)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span></span><span><span>.</span></span></p></div></div></div><p><span><span>These two guesses were unsuccessful: they did not solve the equation </span></span><span><span><span><span><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\def\A{\red{A}}
\def\B{\blue{B}}
\def\C{\green{C}}
\red{-1}x^2 \blue{-1}x + \green{2} = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span></span><span><span>. What would a successful guess — that is, a </span></span><span><b>root </b></span><span><span>— look like on our graph?</span></span></p><div><div><div><p><span><span>It’s the other way around. The </span></span><span><span><span><span><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\def\A{\red{A}}
\def\B{\blue{B}}
\def\C{\green{C}}
y</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span></span><span><span>-axis line is the set of points where </span></span><span><span><span><span><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\def\A{\red{A}}
\def\B{\blue{B}}
\def\C{\green{C}}
x=0</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span></span><span><span>, so a </span></span><span><span><span><span><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext>✘</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\def\A{\red{A}}
\def\B{\blue{B}}
\def\C{\green{C}}
\red{✘}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span></span><span><span> on the </span></span><span><span><span><span><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\def\A{\red{A}}
\def\B{\blue{B}}
\def\C{\green{C}}
y</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span></span><span><span>-axis would just be the guess </span></span><span><span><span><span><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\def\A{\red{A}}
\def\B{\blue{B}}
\def\C{\green{C}}
x=0</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span></span><span><span>.</span></span></p><p><span><span>We’re trying to solve </span></span><span><span><span><span><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\def\A{\red{A}}
\def\B{\blue{B}}
\def\C{\green{C}}
\red{-1}x^2 \blue{-1}x + \green{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span></span><span><span>. We’re plotting </span></span><span><span><span><span><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\def\A{\red{A}}
\def\B{\blue{B}}
\def\C{\green{C}}
y = \red{-1}x^2 \blue{-1}x + \green{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span></span><span><span>, so finding a point where </span></span><span><span><span><span><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\def\A{\red{A}}
\def\B{\blue{B}}
\def\C{\green{C}}
y=0</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span></span><span><span> would solve our equation.</span></span></p><p><span><span>The </span></span><span><span><span><span><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\def\A{\red{A}}
\def\B{\blue{B}}
\def\C{\green{C}}
x</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span></span><span><span>-axis line is the set of points where </span></span><span><span><span><span><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\def\A{\red{A}}
\def\B{\blue{B}}
\def\C{\green{C}}
y=0</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span></span><span><span>. So all </span></span><span><span><span><span><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext>✘</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\def\A{\red{A}}
\def\B{\blue{B}}
